  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nNo significant changes to the forecast thinking with a pair of\nsystems crossing the area tonight through Sunday night as broad\ntrough deepens over the Northeast. The first system will be a\ncompact area of low pressure forming near southern New England\ntomorrow morning and exiting east of Cape Cod during the\nafternoon. Models continue to show a rapid increase of deep\nmoisture tonight into Sunday morning as PWATs climb to 1.5 to 2\ninches. CAMs and global models have now started to show broad\nforcing for ascent leading to an axis of rain extending north \nof the developing low across much of NH late tonight into Sunday\nmorning which has resulted in a slight increase of QPF there. \nThis axis of rain will then become focused across southern NH \ninto the coastal plain of Maine late morning into the afternoon \nas areas to the NW see a break in precipitation. Rain then moves\noffshore by evening.\n\nA cold front will slide east across Upstate NY Sunday evening\nwith showers and storms forming along the front. These showers\nand storms will approach CT Valley after sunset while there is \npotential for lingering MU CAPE to keep chances for thunder in \nthe forecast. Instability wanes overnight with mainly a broken \nline of showers crossing the area with the cold front. Total QPF\nwill range from 0.25 inches north of the Maine mountains and\nincrease to the south and west with an areal average of 0.75\ninches across SW NH. Locally high amounts around 1 inch will be\npossible if some convective processes can be realized with the\ninitial axis of rain late tonight through Sunday morning, mainly\nacross central and southern NH. Rain chances diminish NW to SE\nas the cold front moves offshore Monday morning.\n\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSurface high pressure will arrive from the west on Monday into\nMonday night behind a cold front. This will allow for clearing\nskies during the morning with high temperatures into the 60s to\nlower 70s from north to south. It will be a gusty day with NW\nwind gusts up to around 30-35 mph possible. The combination of\nmainly clear skies and light winds will likely allow for good\nradiational cooling conditions with forecast lows in the 30s \nacross inland valleys with 40s elsewhere. Patchy frost is\npossible across valley locations. \n\nHigh pressure will remain over the forecast area through Tuesday\nnight before shifting offshore by Wednesday. Dry conditions will\npersist with highs on Tuesday into the lower 70s before warming\ninto the middle to upper 70s on Wednesday. Some increase in\ncloud cover will keep lows warmer with readings into the 40s and\n50s. \n\nShower chances then increase Thursday into Thursday night as a\nfrontal boundary and associated weak wave of low pressure drops\nsouth out of Canada. Drier conditions are then possible as we\napproach next weekend.",
